{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of G1, host `none`, at exact head fb080d5934e098a1b93e3cc30de3e19516074b33 (tree 5f7e0a311a4362f61399ea5330eca40e55a09cf0), for request 2b5e3a07 under builder promise 50eb700b. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nI read the implementation note fb12037e first, then the request and sections 3, 4, 9 and 10 of the Git host choice note, then every production change from Main 61fc3e3d. I read the new tests that carry W1 to W5.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- Base and delta. The head descends from Main 61fc3e3d through 15 commits. They change exactly the 94 paths named by the 94 artifacts, one artifact per path, and none of the artifacts has been superseded.\n- Gate. The final gate ran at bdd07948, tree 30120d56. Its receipt records exit 0, identical source before and after, and no collection errors. The output ends \"gate passed\". From bdd07948 to the head, only the note and its manifest change. That meets the document-only follow-up rule in docs/testing.md, so no new gate is owed. The failed first gate (at 290bc809) and the failed limited continuation are kept as failures, and the note does not count them as coverage.\n- Evidence:\n  - The committed manifest has SHA-256 40540564…, as stated.\n  - The collector has 369 files and 187,544,230 bytes. All 369 entries match their recorded size and hash.\n  - No entry is writable.\n  - The root SHA256SUMS has SHA-256 ef61e3d7… and 368 lines, all OK.\n  - The report-tail SHA256SUMS has SHA-256 d0b29ccc…, and all its lines are OK.\n- Controls. Both control logs show the named witness failing by assertion: unpublished.test.ts for the claim result, and host-none.scope.test.ts for the absent-repository guard.\n- Documents:\n  - check-doc-links on an export of the head: 714 links, 0 broken.\n  - check-notes 61fc3e3d..fb080d59: one warning, the 70,929-byte manifest, as the note says.\n  - check-commit-messages: 15 checked, 0 flagged.\n  - `git diff --check`: clean.\n- K1 count. The catalog now has 76 rows, as docs/kernel.md says.\n- Configs. In each of the three Wrangler configs, REGISTER_SCOPE equals that file's ARTIFACTS_CONFIG registerScope.\n\nI reran no suite.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe design follows the note and stays narrow. The unpublished cohort reuses the archive-device-publication family:\n- Directory20 is that family's directory data with the same rules.\n- Destination16 is destination4 data with the destination4 rules.\n- membership@8, rules@10 and inbox@1 are reused unchanged.\n\nThe new parts are small overlays:\n- Register21 checks the host against the closed list (`host-unknown`) and normalizes `none` to the empty namespace. It refuses `archive` or `import` at `found` with `no-repository`. Under `none` it sends `create-directory` straight from `found`.\n- Directory20 makes the repository value optional and guards `bind-archive`.\n- Destination16 adds an `unpublished` branch state. Every act except `establish`, and every receive handler, carries a `has-repository` guard. `declare-first-head` sets `unpublished` when no repository was given.\n\nHistorical definitions, pins and evaluators are not changed.\n\nDownstream code checks for the exact new pins only:\n- delivery preflight, session birth and the session view's three-state branch tuple;\n- the credential route, MCP `credential`, `dap status` and `dap remote`;\n- the Page and the site route.\n\nEach one recognizes an unpublished room only from the exact pin and a fully empty branch, and refuses malformed metadata on older definitions.\n\n`readRegisterScope` is the one selector for creation, child lineage, census and health:\n- REGISTER_SCOPE is primary.\n- A valid Artifacts-only pin remains the fallback.\n- Settings that are supplied but malformed, or that disagree, give `misconfigured`.\n\nThe Worker now routes announcement work through the bridge's own `accepts`. That removes a duplicate owner list, and the routed set is exactly directory@11 to @20.\n\nThe CLI host list is closed and required for new plans. Retained plans keep their recorded host and pin. `--host none` selects the unpublished cohort, and any other explicit cohort is refused before signing. The Page's Start uses one server-rendered boolean for whether DEMO_START_CONFIG is present. With no setting, it shows the CLI instruction and makes no request.\n\nDeployment point for the planner. This is not a defect. A deployment pins one register, and a register's host is fixed for its life. The first host-`none` room therefore needs a Worker whose REGISTER_SCOPE names a new register@21 installed with `--host none`. If that Worker keeps ARTIFACTS_CONFIG, its registerScope must name the same register, or creation answers `misconfigured`. If you re-point an existing Worker, its old register can no longer create rooms (child lineage is bound to the pin), but its existing rooms keep working. The creation-pin baseline already includes the unpublished cohort, so PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS needs no edit.\n\n## Security\n\n- Creation admission keeps its order:\n  - deployment, register pin and signed-install authorization come first;\n  - then the host and cohort checks: `host-unavailable` for `artifacts` without the ARTIFACTS binding, and `unsupported-definition` for `none` on any register other than @21;\n  - all of this happens before the native target lookup.\n- A deployed request with no pin is now refused even when ALLOW_OPEN_CREATION is \"true\". That is stricter than at Main.\n- `misconfigured` and `host-unavailable` are creation answers only. The client accepts them only for `Created`, and they are never native act answers.\n- The credential route refuses `no-repository` only after the holder check passes, so an unauthenticated caller still gets `forbidden`. The object bounds its branch read to 1 MiB and checks the exact shape. MCP `credential` requires a read session before it reads the summary.\n- Register21 binds the claim's `genesis` slot to the exact applied result: the same fact, seq 0, a directory, the original send, and the exact Directory20 seed caused by the `found` intent. This works on both the direct path and the hosted path. The control shows that without this check a forged result is admitted.\n- A refused publication delivery still verifies its source and own-room affiliation before the refusal is recorded. Its result returns to the actual sender. The control shows the guard is what turns the publication into a recorded `no-repository`.\n- The session-view change is narrow. For Destination16 only, it reads the scope captured with the frame instead of seeking again. Final head checks, session authentication and every hard limit stay in place, and no production limit changed.\n- The site route answers an unpublished room with 404 and the plain-text body `no-repository`, without selecting a provider. A room that changes between reads is refused as before.\n- The Page boolean carries no secret, and the CSP and no-cache headers are unchanged.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe change is proportionate: 450 added and 104 removed production lines across 54 files (scripts and configs included), most of them one-line cohort-list additions. Two points, not blocking:\n\n1. The \"exact unpublished branch\" test is written out separately in five places:\n   - commands.ts `destinationStateOf`;\n   - status-report.ts;\n   - MCP `#credential`;\n   - object.ts `credential`;\n   - the Page's `openRoom` (directory form).\n\n   They differ slightly. Some check `token`, some check `name`/`membership`/`rules`, and some require exactly one branch. One shared predicate in dap-platform would keep them aligned when G3 adds `bind-repository`. G3 is the natural place for this.\n2. `dap merge` now reads the destination before the lane, which costs published rooms one extra summary read for each merge. Destination16 already refuses `reserve`, so the early CLI refusal only saves the lane read. This is acceptable as it is.\n\nDestination16 includes `certify-rules-tree` so that the act exists to refuse, as section 4's table requires. Under rules@10 it is optional.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved. The no-repository refusals come after authentication, the creation host and cohort checks come after authorization and before target lookup, the claim result is bound to its exact source, and no production bound changed.\n- Simplification: approved, with the shared-predicate point left for G3.\n\nThis approval covers the software head only. Configuration review, deployment and the first hosted host-`none` room (W1 to W3 on the test Worker) remain the planner's, under G1. The landing check will follow the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fb12037e05847ccd3f2b54370ac43ab44fd51ab8","binding":"assigned","head":"fb080d5934e098a1b93e3cc30de3e19516074b33","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1bf1eee0f94175e1eacbee4162745786078223c7\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:50eb700b344f334c37dfeafcf63032dc7abaa59d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1bf1eee0f94175e1eacbee4162745786078223c7","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}